/*
:name: lambda_reserved_name
:type: error
:error: (is a built-in|may not take that name)
:help: (rename the|not a built-in call)
*/

// BUG (silently dead code): `concat` is a compiler built-in whose call site is
// intercepted UNCONDITIONALLY -- every `concat(a, b)` is rewritten into the
// n-ary LNAST concat node before any lambda lookup happens. A user lambda by
// that name therefore parsed fine, bound nothing, and its body was never
// reached; the failure surfaced far away as an unrelated `concat-untyped-lane`.
// It must be rejected where it is DEFINED.
//
// A method is still fine: `x.concat(…)` has a receiver and is never
// intercepted. Only this top-level source-named shape is an error.
comb concat(a:u3, b:u4) -> (res:u7) {
  res = a
}

comb top(i:u3, j:u4) -> (o:u7) {
  o = concat(i, j)
}
